The **Chrysler Building** is a 1,046-foot-tall (319 m), [Art Deco](link_14)-style [supertall skyscraper](link_15) in the [East Midtown](link_16) neighborhood of [Manhattan](link_6), New York City, United States. Located at the intersection of [42nd Street](link_17) and [Lexington Avenue](link_5), it is the tallest steel-framed brick building in the world. It was both the world's first [supertall skyscraper](link_15) and the [world's tallest building](link_18) for 11 months after its completion in 1930. As of 2019, the Chrysler is the [13th-tallest building in the city](link_19), tied with [The New York Times Building](link_20).[[13]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chrysler_Building#cite_note-13)

Originally a project of real estate developer and former New York State Senator [William H. Reynolds](link_21), the building was commissioned by [Walter Chrysler](link_22), the head of the [Chrysler Corporation](link_0). The construction of the Chrysler Building, an [early skyscraper](link_23), was characterized by a competition with [40 Wall Street](link_1) and the [Empire State Building](link_2) to become the world's tallest building. The Chrysler Building was designed and funded by Walter Chrysler personally as a real estate investment for his children, but it was not intended as the Chrysler Corporation's headquart

